<span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Buying vs Renting</span>
<h1>Expats: Ownership Paperwork Questions for Hanoi Motorbikes</h1>
<p>A motorbike in Hanoi has two bodies, the metal one that carries the rider and the paper one that carries the law, and the paperwork questions are how the expat buyer makes sure both bodies belong to the same machine and the same owner. This guide writes the questions rather than the procedures, because registration and ownership transfer are administrative facts that live with current official sources, change on official schedules, and deserve better than a secondhand paragraph&rsquo;s confidence. The questions, asked in order and answered in writing, are the expat&rsquo;s best legal equipment in the whole transaction.</p>
<p>The machines, the briefing and the honest numbers come from Motorbike Rental - Nguyen Tu, 112 Nguyen Van Cu in Long Bien, Hanoi, open daily 09:00 to 21:00, where the rental paperwork takes minutes and the desk answers ownership questions by pointing at the offices that actually own them. Begin at the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-for-sale/">motorbike for sale hub</a>, study the long-stay arrangements on the <a href="/monthly-motorbike-rental-hanoi/">monthly motorbike rental</a> page, check the verified figures on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">Hanoi motorbike rental cost</a> hub, review the <a href="/motorbike-guides/">motorbike guides</a> for maintenance and safety, and keep <a href="/renting-a-motorbike-in-vietnam/">renting a motorbike in Vietnam</a> open for the legal layer.</p>
